    "note": "# AuraGraph Study Notes\n\n*Proficiency: **Advanced** — Full depth — derivations, formulas, edge-case conditions*\n\n> ⚠️ **Note:** These notes are generated using the `local_summarizer` fallback engine. Add your Azure OpenAI keys to `backend/.env` for true AI-fused notes.\n\n\n---\n\n## Let Xbe A Random Variable Such That\n\n1ifω=success 0ifω=failure Let the success probability denoted by pand failure probability by q= 1−pwith 0< p < 1.\n\n$$\nX(ω) =(\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Write the definition first, then explain with an example for full marks.\n\n---\n\n## The Pmf Of Xis Defined As\n\nIf Xfollows the above PMF, we say Xfollows Bernoulli distribution and denote it by X∼Ber(p).\n\nMoment generating function. =q+pet 4 Binomial Distribution Idea.\n\nConsider nindependent and identical Bernoulli trials each with success probability p.\n\nLet Xbe a random variable denoting the number of success.\n\nThen Xcan take values 0,1, . . . , n.\n\n$$\npX(x) =(\n$$\n\n$$\npifx= 1\n$$\n\n$$\n1−pifx= 0\n$$\n\n$$\nE(X) = 0·(1−p) + 1·p\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Write the definition first, then explain with an example for full marks.\n\n---\n\n## Csl-373: Probability Theory For Computer Engineers\n\n1 Geometric Distribution Idea.\n\nConsider independent and identical (IID) Bernoulli trials (each with success probability p,0< p < 1) till the first success is achieved.\n\nLet Xbe a random variable denoting the number of trials needed for the first success.\n\nThen Xtakes values 1,2,···\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Write the definition first, then explain with an example for full marks.\n\n---\n\n## The Pmf Of Xis Given By\n\nIf Xhas the above PMF, we say Xfollows Geometric distribution and denote it by X∼Geo(p).\n\nShow that V ar(X) =q p2.\n\nUse the identity1 xifor|x|<1.\n\nMoment generating function. i=1eitqi−1p =pet∞X =pet Memoryless property of Geometric distribution.\n\nStarting point does not matter in Geometric distribution.\n\n$$\npX(X=i) =(\n$$\n\n$$\n(1−p)i−1pifi= 1,2, . . .\n$$\n\n$$\nE(X) =∞X\n$$\n\n$$\ni=1iqi−1p\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** State the theorem/definition precisely — examiners award marks for exact wording.\n\n---\n\n## We Have\n\n=qmp∞X =qmp1 P(X > m +n|X > n ) =P({X > m +n} ∩ {X > n }) P(X > n ) P(X > n ) =qn+m 2 Negative Binomial Distribution Idea.\n\nConsider independent and identical Bernoulli trials (each with success probability p,0< p < 1) till the r-th success is achieved where r≥1.\n\nAlso let q=p−1 Let Xbe a random variable denoting the number of trials needed for the r-th success.\n\n$$\nP(X > m ) =∞X\n$$\n\n$$\ni=m+1qi−1p\n$$\n\n$$\ni=1qi−1\n$$\n\n$$\n=qmp(1 +q+q2+···)\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Write the definition first, then explain with an example for full marks.\n\n---\n\n## We Use The Above Result To Show That\n\n> 💡 **Intuition (re: \"_prove it_\"):** To build intuition: try to identify a real-world analogy or work through the smallest possible concrete example first. Focus specifically on what 'prove' means in this context. _The student requires a derivation or proof of the stated result._\n\nFor example, (1) number of students coming to class in the first 10 minutes; (2) number of accidents in a day; (3) number of calls during 1 hour period.\n\nWe define occurrences in a given time interval as a Poisson proces s provided the following assump- tions hold. 1.\n\nNumber of occurrences during the disjoint time intervals are independent. 2.\n\nProbability of a single occurrence during a small time interval is proportional to the length of the interval. [Also called as rate of occurrence] 3.\n\nProbability of more than one occurrence during a small time interval is negligible.\n\nAssumption 2 means P1(h) =pX(X(h) = 1) = λh.\n\nAssumption 3 means =ϵ(h) (ϵ(h)is a negligible function ) Definition 1 (Negligible function) .\n\nWe say fis negligible if for all (positive) polynomials p, there exists n0∈Nsuch that for all n≥n0, Definition 1 says fis asymptotically smaller than the inverse of any polynomial.\n\n$$\nx=0\u0000k\n$$\n\n$$\nE(X) =nX\n$$\n\n$$\nx=0x·\u0000k\n$$\n\n$$\nx=1x·\u0000k\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** State the theorem/definition precisely — examiners award marks for exact wording.\n\n---\n\n## We Use Induction To Prove That\n\nWe need to show that P0(t) =e−λtfor some t >0.\n\nReplace tbyt+hand divide into two disjoint intervals (0, t]and(t, t+h].\n\nThen we have, hP0(t) h= lim h→0\u0010 −λP0(t)−ϵ(h) hP0(t)\u0011 dP0(t) Case n= 1.\n\nWe need to show that P1(t) =e−λtλtfor some t >0.\n\nReplace tbyt+hand divide into two disjoint intervals (0, t]and(t, t+h].\n\nReplace tbyt+hand divide into two disjoint intervals (0, t]and(t, t+h].\n\nThen we have, k!+kX k!+kX h= lim h→0\u0010 dt=e−λt(λt)k Example 2.\n\nThen X∼Poisson (1 3·3)60 60!.\n\n$$\npX(X(t) =n) =e−λt(λt)n\n$$\n\n$$\nn!, λ > 0;n= 0,1, . . . ,\n$$\n\n$$\nP0(t+h) =P(0occurence in (0, t+h])\n$$\n\n$$\n=P({0occurence in (0, t]} ∩ { 0occurence in (t, t+h]})\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Reproduce the derivation step-by-step — partial credit is given for correct intermediate steps.\n\n---\n\n## Hence The Pdf Is Given By\n\nλe−λt, t > 0 0, t ≤0 If a random variable Tfollows the above PDF, we say Thas exponential distribution with parameter λ, and denote it by T∼Exp(λ).\n\nValidity of PDF. 0λe−λtdt 0λe−udu λsetting λt=u 0e−udu k-th order expectation. 0tkλe−λtdt 0tke−λtdt λke−udu λsetting λt=u λkZ∞ 0uke−udu λkZ∞ λk(Gamma function) λk(since kis a positive integer) Setting k= 1, we get Gamma function.\n\nThen the integral 0xα−1e−xdx , is called the Gamma function.\n\nIn particular, if α=nis a positive integer then Γ(n) = (n−1)!.\n\nMoment generating function. 0etuλe−λtdt 0e−t(λ−u)dt 0e−zdz λ−usetting t(λ−u) =z λ−uZ∞ 0e−zdz λ−u,foru < λ Memoryless property of Exponential distribution.\n\nLet a, b > 0.\n\nWe have P(T > b ) e−λb =e−λa Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Roorkee\n\n$$\nfT(t) =(\n$$\n\n$$\n0fT(t)dt=Z∞\n$$\n\n$$\nE(Tk) =Z∞\n$$\n\n$$\n0tkfT(t)dt\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** State the theorem/definition precisely — examiners award marks for exact wording.\n\n---\n\n## Thus,\n\n> 💡 **Intuition (re: \"_explain in detail_\"):** To build intuition: try to identify a real-world analogy or work through the smallest possible concrete example first. Focus specifically on what 'explain' means in this context. _The student requires additional context and an intuitive explanation of this concept._\n\n1.4 Function of a random variable: Continuous case Let Xbe a continuous random variable defined over (Ω,F,P).\n\nSuppose f:R→Ris a measurable function (with respect to σ-fields BRandBR).\n\nLet Xbe a continuous random variable over (Ω,F,P).\n\nSuppose f(x)is a strictly monotonic (increasing or decreasing), differentiable (and thus continuous) function of x.\n\nThen the random variable Y defined by Y=f(X)has a probability density function given by fX(f−1(y))\f\f\f\fdf−1(y) 0, o.w.\n\nLetfbe strictly increasing, sodf(x) dx>0for all x.\n\nSince fis strictly increasing, so is f−1which implies df−1(y) dy>0for all y.\n\n$$\nP(Y=y) =\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X= 0), y = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X=−1) +P(X= 1), y = 1\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X=−2) +P(X= 2), y = 4\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** State the theorem/definition precisely — examiners award marks for exact wording.\n\n---\n\n## Differentiation Gives\n\nFor strictly decreasing function f, the proof is similar and hence omitted.\n\nLet Xbe a continuous random variable defined by 1,0< x < 1 0, o.w. λln(1−X)forλ > 0.\n\nSince Yis a continuous function, so it is a random variable.\n\nAlso, it is strictly increasing and differentiable.\n\nSo Theorem 3 can be applied to compute the PDF of Y. fX(f−1(y))\f\f\f\fdf−1(y) 0, o.w. 1.\f\f\f\fd(1−e−λy) 0, o.w. λe−λy,0< y < ∞ 0, o.w.\n\nCorollary of Theorem 3.\n\nLetfbe a piece-wise strictly monotonic (increasing or decreasing), differentiable (and thus continuous) function of xin intervals I1, I2,···, In.\n\nThen the PDF of Y=f(X)is given by Pn k(y))\f\f\f\f\fdf−1 k(y) 0, o.w. where fkisfrestricted on interval Ik.\n\n$$\nfY(y) =\n$$\n\n$$\nfX(x) =(\n$$\n\n$$\nLetY=−1\n$$\n\n$$\nLetf(x) =y=−1\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** State the theorem/definition precisely — examiners award marks for exact wording.\n\n---\n\n## And Statistics For Engineers *(Textbook)*\n\nSoong State University of New York at Buffalo, Buffalo, New York, USA TLFeBOOK TLFeBOOK\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Write the definition first, then explain with an example for full marks.\n\n---\n\n## West Sussex Po19 8Sq, England *(Textbook)*\n\nTelephone ( 44) 1243 779777 Email (for orders and customer service enquiries): cs-books@wiley.co.uk Visit our Home Page on www.wileye urope.com or www.wiley.com All Rights Reserved.\n\nNo part of this publication may be reproduced, stored in a retrievalsystem or transmitted in any form or by any means, electronic, mechanical, photocopying, recording, scanning or otherwise, except under the terms of the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 or under the terms of a licence issued by the Copyright Licensing Agency Ltd, 90 Tottenham Court Road, London W1T 4LP, UK, without the permission in writing of the Publisher.\n\nRequests to the Publisher should be addressed to the Permissions Department, John Wiley & Sons Ltd, The Atrium, Southern Gate, Chichester, West Sussex PO19 8SQ,England, or emailed to permreq@wiley.co.uk, or faxed to ( 44) 1243 770620.\n\nThis publication is designed to provide accurate and authoritative information in regard to the subject matter covered.\n\nIt is sold on the understanding that the Publisher is not engaged inrendering professional services.\n\nIf professional advice or other expert assistance is required, the services of a competent professional should be sought.\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Know and state the conditions/constraints — often 1–2 marks in exams.\n\n---\n\n## Other Wiley Editorial Offices *(Textbook)*\n\nJohn Wiley & Sons Inc., 111 River Street, Hoboken, NJ 07030, USA Jossey-Bass, 989 Market Street, San Francisco, CA 94103-1741, USA Wiley-VCH Verlag GmbH, Boschstr. 12, D-69