  {
    "id": "418fb935-fa58-4a7e-8f19-db769df8f05c",
    "user_id": "f4452e08-7b0c-4bfd-abe9-4d9a945c2c04",
    "name": "prob",
    "course": "prob",
    "note": "## Measurable Functions\n\n### What is a Measurable Function?\n\nA measurable function is a function that \"respects\" the structure of measurable spaces. It ensures that the pre-image of any measurable set is also measurable. This concept is crucial in real analysis and probability theory, as it allows us to define and work with random variables in a rigorous manner.\n\n> Imagine you have two rooms, each with a set of rules about what counts as \"clean\" or \"dirty.\" A measurable function is like a cleaning robot that moves between the rooms, ensuring that anything it brings from one room to the other follows the rules of cleanliness in both rooms.\n\n#### Formal Definition:\nLet $(\\Omega_1, F_1)$ and $(\\Omega_2, F_2)$ be measurable spaces. A function $f: \\Omega_1 \\to \\Omega_2$ is said to be measurable (with respect to the $\\sigma$-fields $F_1$ and $F_2$) if:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(A) \\in F_1, \\quad \\text{for all } A \\in F_2.\n$$\n\n| Symbol      | What it means                          | Typical units/range             |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| $\\Omega_1$ | Domain of the function $f$ | Set of all possible inputs |\n| $\\Omega_2$ | Codomain of the function $f$ | Set of all possible outputs |\n| $F_1$ | $\\sigma$-field over $\\Omega_1$ | Collection of measurable subsets |\n| $F_2$ | $\\sigma$-field over $\\Omega_2$ | Collection of measurable subsets |\n| $f^{-1}(A)$ | Pre-image of set $A$ under $f$ | Subset of $\\Omega_1$ |\n\nThis definition says that for any measurable set $A$ in the codomain $\\Omega_2$, the set of all points in the domain $\\Omega_1$ that map into $A$ under $f$ must also be measurable.\n\n### Pre-Image of a Set\n\nThe pre-image of a set under a function is the set of all inputs that map into the given set.\n\n> Think of a vending machine. The pre-image of \"chips\" is the set of all buttons you can press to get chips.\n\n#### Formal Definition:\nFor $f: T \\to U$ and $E \\subseteq U$, the pre-image of $E$ under $f$ is:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(E) = \\{t \\in T \\mid f(t) \\in E\\}.\n$$\n\n| Symbol      | What it means                          | Typical units/range             |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| $T$ | Domain of the function $f$ | Set of all possible inputs |\n| $U$ | Codomain of the function $f$ | Set of all possible outputs |\n| $E$ | Subset of $U$ | Measurable set in the codomain |\n| $f^{-1}(E)$ | Pre-image of $E$ under $f$ | Subset of $T$ |\n\nThis formula tells us how to find the subset of the domain $T$ that corresponds to a given subset $E$ of the codomain $U$.\n\n### Measurable Sets\n\nA measurable set is a subset of a measurable space that satisfies the rules of the $\\sigma$-field.\n\n> Imagine a library where only certain books are allowed to be borrowed. A measurable set is like the collection of books that follow the borrowing rules.\n\n#### Formal Definition:\nLet $(\\Omega, F)$ be a measurable space. A set $E \\subseteq \\Omega$ is said to be measurable if:\n\n$$\nE \\in F.\n$$\n\n| Symbol      | What it means                          | Typical units/range             |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| $\\Omega$ | The space | Set of all possible elements |\n| $F$ | $\\sigma$-field over $\\Omega$ | Collection of measurable subsets |\n| $E$ | Subset of $\\Omega$ | Measurable set |\n\nThis definition ensures that $E$ follows the rules of the $\\sigma$-field $F$.\n\n### Borel $\\sigma$-Field\n\nThe Borel $\\sigma$-field is the collection of all measurable subsets of the real line.\n\n> Think of the real line as a city map. The Borel $\\sigma$-field is like the list of all neighborhoods you can visit.\n\n#### Definition:\nThe Borel $\\sigma$-field, denoted $B\\mathbb{R}$, is the $\\sigma$-field generated by the open subsets of the real line $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n### Measurable Functions and Random Variables\n\n#### Example 1: Random Variable\nA random variable $X$ is a measurable function because it maps events in a probability space to measurable subsets of the real line.\n\n> Imagine rolling a die. The random variable $X$ maps the outcome (e.g., \"roll 4\") to a measurable subset of the real line (e.g., $[4, 4]$).\n\nLet $\\Omega_1 = \\Omega$, $\\Omega_2 = \\mathbb{R}$, $F_1 = \\text{power set of } \\Omega$, and $F_2 = B\\mathbb{R}$. Define $f: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{R}$ as the random variable $X$, i.e., $f := X$. Then:\n\n$$\nX^{-1}((-\\infty, x]) \\in F_1, \\quad \\text{for all } (-\\infty, x] \\in F_2.\n$$\n\n| Symbol      | What it means                          | Typical units/range             |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| $\\Omega$ | Sample space | Set of all possible outcomes |\n| $\\mathbb{R}$ | Real line | Set of all real numbers |\n| $F_1$ | $\\sigma$-field over $\\Omega$ | Collection of measurable subsets |\n| $F_2$ | Borel $\\sigma$-field over $\\mathbb{R}$ | Collection of measurable subsets |\n| $X^{-1}$ | Pre-image under $X$ | Subset of $\\Omega$ |\n\nThis example shows that random variables are measurable functions because their pre-images of measurable sets are measurable.\n\n#### Example 2: Constant Function\nLet $(\\Omega, F)$ and $(\\mathbb{R}, B\\mathbb{R})$ be measurable spaces. Define $X: \\Omega \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by:\n\n$$\nX(\\omega) = c, \\quad \\text{for all } \\omega \\in \\Omega.\n$$\n\nFor all $A \\in B\\mathbb{R}$, we have:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(A) =\n\\begin{cases} \n\\emptyset, & c \\notin A, \\\\\n\\Omega, & c \\in A.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol      | What it means                          | Typical units/range             |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| $\\Omega$ | Sample space | Set of all possible outcomes |\n| $F$ | $\\sigma$-field over $\\Omega$ | Collection of measurable subsets |\n| $\\mathbb{R}$ | Real line | Set of all real numbers |\n| $B\\mathbb{R}$ | Borel $\\sigma$-field over $\\mathbb{R}$ | Collection of measurable subsets |\n| $c$ | Constant value | Fixed real number |\n\nSince $\\emptyset \\in F$ and $\\Omega \\in F$ for all $A \\in B\\mathbb{R}$, $X$ is a measurable function.\n\n> **Exam Tip:** Always check whether the pre-image of a set under a function belongs to the $\\sigma$-field of the domain. This is the key property of a measurable function.\n\n## Function of a Random Variable\n\n![The diagram is a block diagram or flowchart illustrating a function composition. Key components include a circle labeled $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathcal{P})$, functions $X(\\cdot)$, $f(\\cdot)$, and $f \\circ X(\\cdot)$, and a mapping $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$. It illustrates the concept of a random variable $X$ composed with a function $f$.](/api/images/418fb935-fa58-4a7e-8f19-db769df8f05c/img_001.png)\n*The diagram is a block diagram or flowchart illustrating a function composition. Key components include a circle labeled $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathcal{P})$, functions $X(\\cdot)$, $f(\\cdot)$, and $f \\circ X(\\cdot)$, and a mapping $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$. It illustrates the concept of a random variable $X$ composed with a function $f$.*\n\n![The image shows a 3D surface plot of a joint probability distribution function $f_{xy}(x,y)$. The key component visible is the 3D surface plot. The concept illustrated is likely joint probability distributions.](/api/images/418fb935-fa58-4a7e-8f19-db769df8f05c/tb_img_001.jpg)\n*The image shows a 3D surface plot of a joint probability distribution function $f_{xy}(x,y)$. The key component visible is the 3D surface plot. The concept illustrated is likely joint probability distributions.*\n\n\n### Introduction to Function of a Random Variable\nA function of a random variable involves transforming one random variable into another using a mathematical function. If $X$ is a random variable and $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a measurable function, then $Y = f(X)$ is also a random variable.\n\n> Imagine you have a random variable $X$ representing the temperature in Celsius. If you apply the function $f(X) = X \\times 9/5 + 32$, you transform $X$ into $Y$, which represents the temperature in Fahrenheit. The randomness of $X$ carries over to $Y$.\n\n#### Formal Definition:\nLet $X$ be a random variable defined over $(\\Omega, F, P)$, and let $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a measurable function. Then $Y = f(X)$ is a random variable. \n\n### Proof: $Y = f(X)$ is a Random Variable\nTo prove that $Y = f(X)$ is a random variable, it suffices to show that $Y^{-1}((-\\infty, y]) \\in F$ for all $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n#### Steps:\n1. Fix $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n2. The pre-image of the interval $(-\\infty, y]$ under $Y$ is:\n\n   $$\n   Y^{-1}((-\\infty, y]) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid Y(\\omega) \\leq y\\}.\n   $$\n\n3. Substituting $Y = f(X)$:\n\n   $$\n   Y^{-1}((-\\infty, y]) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid f(X(\\omega)) \\leq y\\}.\n   $$\n\n4. This can be rewritten as:\n\n   $$\n   Y^{-1}((-\\infty, y]) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid X(\\omega) \\in f^{-1}((-\\infty, y])\\}.\n   $$\n\n5. Since $f$ is measurable, $f^{-1}((-\\infty, y]) \\in \\mathcal{B}_\\mathbb{R}$ (the Borel $\\sigma$-field).\n6. Since $X$ is a random variable, $X^{-1}(A) \\in F$ for any $A \\in \\mathcal{B}_\\mathbb{R}$. Thus:\n\n   $$\n   X^{-1}(f^{-1}((-\\infty, y])) \\in F.\n   $$\n\n7. Therefore, $Y^{-1}((-\\infty, y]) \\in F$, proving that $Y$ is a random variable.\n\n### Remark on Measurable Functions\nEvery continuous function or piecewise continuous function (defined from $\\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$) is a measurable function.\n\n### Distribution of $Y = f(X)$\nThe distribution of $Y = f(X)$ is fully determined by the distribution of $X$ and the function $f$. The cumulative distribution function (CDF) of $Y$ is given by:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = P(Y \\leq y) = P(f(X) \\leq y) = P(X \\in f^{-1}((-\\infty, y])).\n$$\n\n#### Example 1: CDF of $Y = |X|$\nLet $Y = |X|$. The CDF of $Y$ is:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = \n\\begin{cases} \n0, & y < 0, \\\\\nF_X(y) - F_X(-y), & y \\geq 0.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $F_Y(y)$ | CDF of $Y$ | $[0, 1]$ |\n| $F_X(y)$ | CDF of $X$ | $[0, 1]$ |\n\n#### Example 2: CDF of $Y = aX + b$\nLet $Y = aX + b$ where $a \\neq 0$. The CDF of $Y$ is:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = \n\\begin{cases} \nF_X\\left(\\frac{y - b}{a}\\right), & a > 0, \\\\\n1 - F_X\\left(\\frac{y - b}{a}\\right), & a < 0.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $F_Y(y)$ | CDF of $Y$ | $[0, 1]$ |\n| $F_X(y)$ | CDF of $X$ | $[0, 1]$ |\n\n### Function of a Random Variable: Discrete Case\nLet $X$ be a discrete random variable and $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a measurable function. Define the set:\n\n$$\nB_y = f^{-1}(y) = \\{x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_k \\mid x_i \\in \\mathbb{R} \\text{ and } X(\\omega) = x_i \\text{ for some } \\omega \\in \\Omega\\}.\n$$\n\nThe probability $P(Y = y)$ is computed as:\n\n$$\nP(Y = y) = \\sum_{x \\in B_y} P(X = x).\n$$\n\n#### Example 3: PMF of $Y = X^2$\nLet $X$ have the following PMF:\n\n$$\np_X(x) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\frac{1}{5}, & x = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, \\\\\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nLet $f(X) = X^2$. Then:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(y) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\{0\\}, & y = 0, \\\\\n\\{-1, 1\\}, & y = 1, \\\\\n\\{-2, 2\\}, & y = 4.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe PMF of $Y$ is:\n\n$$\nP(Y = y) = \n\\begin{cases} \nP(X = 0) = \\frac{1}{5}, & y = 0, \\\\\nP(X = -1) + P(X = 1) = \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}, & y = 1, \\\\\nP(X = -2) + P(X = 2) = \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}, & y = 4.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n### Function of a Random Variable: Continuous Case\nLet $X$ be a continuous random variable and $f(x)$ be a strictly monotonic, differentiable, and continuous function. The PDF of $Y = f(X)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \n\\begin{cases} \nf_X(f^{-1}(y)) \\left| \\frac{d}{dy} f^{-1}(y) \\right|, & y = f(x) \\text{ for some } x, \\\\\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n#### Example 4: PDF of $Y = -\\frac{1}{\\lambda} \\ln(1 - X)$\nLet $X$ be uniform on $(0, 1)$ and $Y = -\\frac{1}{\\lambda} \\ln(1 - X)$ for $\\lambda > 0$. Since $Y$ is strictly increasing and differentiable, we apply the formula:\n1. Compute $f^{-1}(y)$:\n\n   $$\n   f^{-1}(y) = 1 - e^{-\\lambda y}.\n   $$\n\n2. Compute the derivative:\n\n   $$\n   \\frac{d}{dy} f^{-1}(y) = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda y}.\n   $$\n\n3. Substitute into the formula for $f_Y(y)$:\n\n   $$\n   f_Y(y) = \n   \\begin{cases} \n   \\lambda e^{-\\lambda y}, & y > 0, \\\\\n   0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   $$\n\n#### Corollary: Piecewise Strictly Monotonic Functions\nIf $f(x)$ is piecewise strictly monotonic, the PDF of $Y = f(X)$ is computed by summing contributions from each monotonic interval:\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \\sum_{k=1}^n f_X(f_k^{-1}(y)) \\left| \\frac{d}{dy} f_k^{-1}(y) \\right|,\n$$\n\nwhere $f_k$ is $f$ restricted to interval $I_k$.\n\n### Exercises\n1. Compute the CDF of:\n   - $Y = X^2$\n   - $Y = \\max\\{X, 0\\}$\n   - $Y = \\min\\{X, 0\\}$\n2. Find a measurable function $f$ for which $B_y$ is countably infinite, or show that no such $f$ exists.\n\n> **Exam Tip:** Always verify whether the function $f$ is strictly monotonic when applying the formula for continuous random variables. Misidentifying monotonicity is a common mistake.",
