  },
  {
    "id": "79df25ea-f793-4bb5-8c82-e2def53d3dc2",
    "user_id": "f4452e08-7b0c-4bfd-abe9-4d9a945c2c04",
    "name": "distributions-1",
    "course": "prob",
    "note": "## Geometric Distribution\n\n### What is the Geometric Distribution?\n\nSimply put, the geometric distribution models the number of independent and identical trials needed to achieve the first success in a sequence of Bernoulli trials, where each trial has the same probability of success, $p$.\n\n> Imagine flipping a coin until you get heads for the first time. If the coin is fair, the probability of heads is $p = 0.5$. The geometric distribution tells us the likelihood of needing a certain number of flips to get that first heads.\n\n#### Formal Definition:\nLet $X$ be the random variable representing the number of trials needed for the first success. If $X$ follows the geometric distribution, we write $X \\sim \\text{Geo}(p)$, where $p$ is the probability of success in each trial ($0 < p < 1$). The possible values of $X$ are $1, 2, 3, \\dots$.\n\n---\n\n### Probability Mass Function (PMF)\n\nThe PMF of the geometric distribution is:\n\n$$\nP(X = i) = (1-p)^{i-1}p \\quad \\text{for } i = 1, 2, \\dots; \\quad P(X = i) = 0 \\quad \\text{otherwise.}\n$$\n\n| Symbol  | What it means                              | Typical units/range |\n|---------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $X$ | Number of trials needed for first success | Positive integers |\n| $p$ | Probability of success in each trial | $0 < p < 1$ |\n| $i$ | Specific number of trials | $i = 1, 2, \\dots$ |\n\nThis formula says that the probability of needing $i$ trials for the first success is proportional to the probability of failing $(i-1)$ times and then succeeding on the $i$th trial.\n\n#### Example:\nSuppose the probability of success in each trial is $p = 0.3$. What is the probability of needing exactly 4 trials for the first success?\n\n$$\nP(X = 4) = (1-0.3)^{4-1} \\cdot 0.3 = (0.7)^3 \\cdot 0.3\n$$\n\nStep-by-step calculation:\n1. Compute $(1-p)$: $1 - 0.3 = 0.7$.\n2. Raise $0.7$ to the power of $3$: $0.7^3 = 0.343$.\n3. Multiply by $p$: $0.343 \\cdot 0.3 = 0.1029$.\n\nThus, $P(X = 4) = 0.1029$. There is approximately a 10.29% chance of needing exactly 4 trials.\n\n---\n\n### Expectation (Mean)\n\nThe expected value (mean) of the geometric distribution is:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{1}{p}.\n$$\n\n| Symbol  | What it means                              | Typical units/range |\n|---------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $E(X)$ | Expected number of trials for first success | Positive real number |\n| $p$ | Probability of success in each trial | $0 < p < 1$ |\n\nThis formula shows that the average number of trials needed for the first success decreases as $p$ increases.\n\n#### Example:\nIf $p = 0.2$, the expected number of trials is:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{1}{0.2} = 5.\n$$\n\nOn average, 5 trials are needed to achieve the first success.\n\n---\n\n### Variance\n\nThe variance of the geometric distribution is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{1-p}{p^2}.\n$$\n\n| Symbol         | What it means                              | Typical units/range |\n|----------------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $\\text{Var}(X)$ | Variance of the number of trials | Positive real number |\n| $p$ | Probability of success in each trial | $0 < p < 1$ |\n\nThis formula indicates how spread out the possible values of $X$ are. A smaller $p$ leads to a larger variance, meaning the number of trials needed for the first success is more variable.\n\n#### Example:\nIf $p = 0.4$, the variance is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{1-0.4}{0.4^2} = \\frac{0.6}{0.16} = 3.75.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Moment Generating Function (MGF)\n\nThe moment generating function of the geometric distribution is:\n\n$$\nM(t) = \\frac{pe^t}{1 - (1-p)e^t} \\quad \\text{for } t < -\\log(1-p).\n$$\n\n| Symbol  | What it means                              | Typical units/range |\n|---------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $M(t)$ | Moment generating function | Function of $t$ |\n| $p$ | Probability of success in each trial | $0 < p < 1$ |\n| $t$ | Parameter for the MGF | $t < -\\log(1-p)$ |\n\nThe MGF helps derive moments (like mean and variance) of the distribution. The condition $t < -\\log(1-p)$ ensures the function is well-defined.\n\n#### Example:\nIf $p = 0.5$, find the MGF for $t = -0.5$.\n\n1. Compute $1-p$: $1 - 0.5 = 0.5$.\n2. Compute $e^t$: $e^{-0.5} \\approx 0.6065$.\n3. Check the condition: $t < -\\log(1-p)$, so $-0.5 < -\\log(0.5) \\approx -0.693$, which is valid.\n4. Substitute into the formula:\n\n$$\nM(-0.5) = \\frac{0.5 \\cdot 0.6065}{1 - (0.5 \\cdot 0.6065)} = \\frac{0.30325}{1 - 0.30325} = \\frac{0.30325}{0.69675} \\approx 0.435.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Memoryless Property\n\nThe geometric distribution has a unique property called the memoryless property. Simply put, the probability of needing additional trials does not depend on how many trials have already occurred.\n\n#### Formal Statement:\n\n$$\nP(X > m+n \\mid X > n) = P(X > m).\n$$\n\n| Symbol  | What it means                              | Typical units/range |\n|---------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $X$ | Number of trials needed for first success | Positive integers |\n| $m, n$ | Number of trials | Positive integers |\n\nThis property means that the distribution \"resets\" after each trial. The starting point does not matter.\n\n#### Example:\nIf $p = 0.3$, find $P(X > 5 \\mid X > 3)$.\n\nUsing the memoryless property:\n\n$$\nP(X > 5 \\mid X > 3) = P(X > 2).\n$$\n\nCompute $P(X > 2)$:\n\n$$\nP(X > 2) = 1 - P(X = 1) - P(X = 2).\n$$\n\n1. Compute $P(X = 1)$: $(1-0.3)^0 \\cdot 0.3 = 0.3$.\n2. Compute $P(X = 2)$: $(1-0.3)^1 \\cdot 0.3 = 0.7 \\cdot 0.3 = 0.21$.\n3. Compute $P(X > 2)$: $1 - 0.3 - 0.21 = 0.49$.\n\nThus, $P(X > 5 \\mid X > 3) = 0.49$.\n\n---\n\n> **Exam Tip:** The memoryless property is a common exam question. Remember that the probability of needing additional trials depends only on the success probability $p$, not on past trials.\n\n## Negative Binomial Distribution\n\n### Introduction to Negative Binomial Distribution\nSimply put, the Negative Binomial distribution models the number of trials needed to achieve a fixed number of successes in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials, where each trial has the same probability of success.\n\n> Imagine flipping a coin repeatedly until you get a certain number of heads (successes). If you want exactly 3 heads, the Negative Binomial distribution helps you determine the probability of needing a specific number of flips to achieve this.\n\nFormally, consider independent and identical Bernoulli trials, each with success probability $p$ (where $0 < p < 1$). Let $r$ be the fixed number of successes desired ($r \\geq 1$). Let $X$ be the random variable representing the number of trials needed to achieve the $r$-th success. Then $X$ follows a Negative Binomial distribution, denoted as $X \\sim \\text{NB}(p, r)$.\n\n### Probability Mass Function (PMF)\nThe PMF of the Negative Binomial distribution is given by:\n\n$$\nP(X = k) = \\binom{k-1}{r-1} (1-p)^{k-r} p^r \\quad \\text{for } k = r, r+1, r+2, \\ldots\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X = k) = 0 \\quad \\text{otherwise.}\n$$\n\n#### Symbol Table\n| Symbol       | What it means                          | Typical units/range |\n|--------------|----------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $X$ | Number of trials needed for $r$ successes | $k \\geq r$ |\n| $k$ | Specific number of trials | Integer ($r, r+1, \\ldots$) |\n| $r$ | Fixed number of successes | Integer ($\\geq 1$) |\n| $p$ | Probability of success in each trial | $0 < p < 1$ |\n| $1-p$ or $q$ | Probability of failure in each trial | $0 < q < 1$ |\n\n#### Explanation\nThis formula calculates the probability of needing exactly $k$ trials to achieve $r$ successes. The term $\\binom{k-1}{r-1}$ accounts for the number of ways to arrange $r-1$ successes among the first $k-1$ trials, and $(1-p)^{k-r} p^r$ accounts for the probabilities of failures and successes.\n\n#### Example\nSuppose $p = 0.5$ (success probability), $r = 3$ (desired successes), and $k = 5$ (trials needed). Calculate $P(X = 5)$:\n\n$$\nP(X = 5) = \\binom{5-1}{3-1} (1-0.5)^{5-3} (0.5)^3\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X = 5) = \\binom{4}{2} (0.5)^2 (0.5)^3\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X = 5) = 6 \\cdot 0.25 \\cdot 0.125 = 0.1875\n$$\n\nThus, the probability of needing exactly 5 trials to achieve 3 successes is $0.1875$.\n\n### Expectation and Variance\nThe expectation and variance of $X$ are:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{r}{p}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{r(1-p)}{p^2}\n$$\n\n#### Symbol Table\n| Symbol       | What it means                          | Typical units/range |\n|--------------|----------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $E(X)$ | Expected number of trials | Real number |\n| $\\text{Var}(X)$ | Variance of the number of trials | Real number |\n| $r$ | Fixed number of successes | Integer ($\\geq 1$) |\n| $p$ | Probability of success in each trial | $0 < p < 1$ |\n| $1-p$ or $q$ | Probability of failure in each trial | $0 < q < 1$ |\n\n#### Explanation\n- $E(X)$ represents the average number of trials needed to achieve $r$ successes.\n- $\\text{Var}(X)$ measures the spread or variability in the number of trials needed.\n\n#### Example\nLet $r = 3$ and $p = 0.5$. Calculate $E(X)$ and $\\text{Var}(X)$:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{3}{0.5} = 6\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{3(1-0.5)}{0.5^2} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 0.5}{0.25} = 6\n$$\n\nThus, the expected number of trials is $6$, and the variance is also $6$.\n\n### Moment Generating Function (MGF)\nThe moment generating function of $X$ is:\n\n$$\nM(t) = \\frac{(p e^t)^r}{(1 - (1-p)e^t)^r} \\quad \\text{for } t < -\\log(1-p).\n$$\n\n#### Symbol Table\n| Symbol       | What it means                          | Typical units/range |\n|--------------|----------------------------------------|---------------------|\n| $M(t)$ | Moment generating function | Function |\n| $t$ | Parameter for the MGF | Real number |\n| $p$ | Probability of success in each trial | $0 < p < 1$ |\n| $1-p$ or $q$ | Probability of failure in each trial | $0 < q < 1$ |\n| $r$ | Fixed number of successes | Integer ($\\geq 1$) |\n\n#### Explanation\nThe MGF is a tool used to derive moments (e.g., mean, variance) of the distribution. The condition $t < -\\log(1-p)$ ensures the function is well-defined.\n\n#### Example\nLet $p = 0.5$, $r = 3$, and $t = -0.5$. Verify $M(t)$:\n\n$$\nM(-0.5) = \\frac{(0.5 e^{-0.5})^3}{(1 - (1-0.5)e^{-0.5})^3}\n$$\n\n$$\nM(-0.5) = \\frac{(0.5 \\cdot 0.6065)^3}{(1 - 0.5 \\cdot 0.6065)^3}\n$$\n\n$$\nM(-0.5) = \\frac{(0.30325)^3}{(1 - 0.30325)^3} = \\frac{0.0279}{0.69675^3} = 0.0576\n$$\n\nThus, $M(-0.5) \\approx 0.0576$.\n\n### Differences Between Binomial and Negative Binomial Distributions\n- **Binomial Distribution**: Fixes the number of trials ($n$) and counts the number of successes ($i$) within those trials.\n- **Negative Binomial Distribution**: Fixes the number of successes ($r$) and counts the number of trials ($k$) needed to achieve those successes.\n\n#### Example\nIf a coin is flipped 10 times, and we count the number of heads, this is a Binomial distribution. If we flip the coin until we get 3 heads, this is a Negative Binomial distribution.\n\n### Exercises\n#### Exercise 1\nUsing the definition of expectation and variance, show that:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{r}{p}, \\quad \\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{r(1-p)}{p^2}.\n$$\n\n#### Exercise 2\nLet $X \\sim \\text{NB}(p, r)$. Verify whether:\n\n$$\nP(X > m+n \\mid X > n) = P(X > n).\n$$\n\n> Exam Tip: The most common mistake is confusing the Negative Binomial distribution with the Binomial distribution. Always remember: Negative Binomial counts trials for a fixed number of successes, while Binomial counts successes for a fixed number of trials.\n\n## Hypergeometric Distribution\n\n### Introduction\nSimply put, the hypergeometric distribution is used to model scenarios where sampling is done **without replacement**. This means the probability of success changes after each selection because the total number of items decreases.\n\n> Imagine you have a jar with 10 red balls and 20 blue balls. If you randomly pick 5 balls without putting them back, the probability of picking a red ball changes after each draw because the total number of balls left in the jar decreases.\n\n### Formal Definition\nThe hypergeometric distribution describes the probability of selecting a certain number of \"Type-I\" items (successes) when sampling **without replacement** from a population containing two types of items. \n\nLet:\n- $N$: Total number of items in the population.\n- $k$: Number of Type-I items (successes) in the population.\n- $N-k$: Number of Type-II items (failures) in the population.\n- $n$: Number of items selected (sample size).\n- $X$: Random variable denoting the number of Type-I items in the sample.\n\nIf $X$ follows the hypergeometric distribution, we write:\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\text{HyperGeo}(N, k, n)\n$$\n\n### Probability Mass Function (PMF)\nThe PMF of the hypergeometric distribution is given by:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\frac{\\binom{k}{x} \\binom{N-k}{n-x}}{\\binom{N}{n}}, & \\text{if } \\max\\{0, n+k-N\\} \\leq x \\leq \\min\\{n, k\\}, \\\\\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $N$ | Total number of items in the population | Positive integer |\n| $k$ | Number of Type-I items (successes) | $0 \\leq k \\leq N$ |\n| $n$ | Number of items selected (sample size) | $0 \\leq n \\leq N$ |\n| $x$ | Number of Type-I items in the sample | $\\max\\{0, n+k-N\\} \\leq x \\leq \\min\\{n, k\\}$ |\n\nThis formula calculates the probability of selecting exactly $x$ Type-I items in the sample. It uses combinations:\n- $\\binom{k}{x}$: Ways to choose $x$ Type-I items from $k$.\n- $\\binom{N-k}{n-x}$: Ways to choose $n-x$ Type-II items from $N-k$.\n- $\\binom{N}{n}$: Total ways to choose $n$ items from $N$.\n\n#### Example: Calculating PMF\nSuppose a population has $N = 20$ items, $k = 15$ Type-I items, and $n = 6$ items are selected. What is the probability of selecting $x = 4$ Type-I items?\n\nStep-by-step:\n1. Compute $\\binom{k}{x}$:\n\n   $$\n   \\binom{15}{4} = \\frac{15 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 12}{4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = 1365\n   $$\n\n2. Compute $\\binom{N-k}{n-x}$:\n\n   $$\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{2 \\cdot 1} = 10\n   $$\n\n3. Compute $\\binom{N}{n}$:\n\n   $$\n   \\binom{20}{6} = \\frac{20 \\cdot 19 \\cdot 18 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 16 \\cdot 15}{6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = 38760\n   $$\n\n4. Plug values into the PMF:\n\n   $$\n   P(X = 4) = \\frac{\\binom{15}{4} \\binom{5}{2}}{\\binom{20}{6}} = \\frac{1365 \\cdot 10}{38760} = 0.352\n   $$\n\nThus, the probability of selecting exactly 4 Type-I items is $0.352$.\n\n### Conditions on $x$\nThe value of $x$ must satisfy:\n1. $x \\leq \\min\\{n, k\\}$: $x$ cannot exceed the total number of Type-I items ($k$) or the sample size ($n$).\n2. $x \\geq \\max\\{0, n+k-N\\}$: $x$ must be at least the number of Type-I items required to ensure the sample size is met.\n\nThese conditions ensure that $x$ is a valid count of Type-I items in the sample.\n\n### Expectation and Variance\n#### Expectation\nThe expected number of Type-I items in the sample is:\n\n$$\nE(X) = n \\cdot \\frac{k}{N}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $E(X)$ | Expected value of $X$ | Real number |\n| $n$ | Sample size | Positive integer |\n| $k$ | Number of Type-I items | $0 \\leq k \\leq N$ |\n| $N$ | Total number of items | Positive integer |\n\nThis formula says that the expected number of successes is proportional to the sample size and the fraction of Type-I items in the population.\n\n#### Variance\nThe variance of $X$ is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = n \\cdot \\frac{k}{N} \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{N}\\right) \\cdot \\frac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $\\text{Var}(X)$ | Variance of $X$ | Real number |\n| $n$ | Sample size | Positive integer |\n| $k$ | Number of Type-I items | $0 \\leq k \\leq N$ |\n| $N$ | Total number of items | Positive integer |\n\nThis formula accounts for the changing probabilities due to sampling without replacement.\n\n#### Exercise\nProve that:\n\n$$\nE(X(X-1)) = \\frac{k(k-1)n(n-1)}{N(N-1)}\n$$\n\nand use this result to show:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{N-n}{N-1} \\cdot \\frac{kn}{N} \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{N}\\right)\n$$\n\n### Convergence to Binomial Distribution\nIf the population size $N \\to \\infty$ and the proportion of Type-I items $k/N \\to p$, the hypergeometric distribution converges to the binomial distribution with parameters $n$ and $p$. Formally:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) \\to \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nThis happens because, as $N$ becomes very large, the effect of sampling without replacement becomes negligible, making the trials effectively independent.\n\n### Worked Example: Functional System Probability\nA system requires at least 4 working components out of 6 selected from a bin of 20 components, where 15 are working. What is the probability the system is functional?\n\nHere:\n- $N = 20$, $k = 15$, $n = 6$, $X$: Number of working components.\n\nWe need $P(X \\geq 4)$:\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 4) = P(X = 4) + P(X = 5) + P(X = 6)\n$$\n\nCompute each term:\n1. $P(X = 4) = \\frac{\\binom{15}{4} \\binom{5}{2}}{\\binom{20}{6}} \\approx 0.352$\n2. $P(X = 5) = \\frac{\\binom{15}{5} \\binom{5}{1}}{\\binom{20}{6}} \\approx 0.441$\n3. $P(X = 6) = \\frac{\\binom{15}{6} \\binom{5}{0}}{\\binom{20}{6}} \\approx 0.076$\n\nAdd probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 4) = 0.352 + 0.441 + 0.076 = 0.869\n$$\n\nThus, the probability the system is functional is approximately $0.869$.\n\n---\n\n> **Exam Tip:** The most common mistake is forgetting that hypergeometric sampling is **without replacement**. Always check the conditions on $x$ and ensure you compute combinations correctly!\n\n## Poisson Process\n\n### Introduction to Poisson Process\n\nSimply put, a Poisson process is a mathematical model used to describe the number of occurrences of an event within a given time interval. It is particularly useful when the events happen randomly but at a constant average rate.\n\n> Imagine you are counting the number of cars passing through a toll booth in one hour. The cars arrive randomly, but there is an average rate at which cars pass through the booth. This situation can be modeled using a Poisson process.\n\nFormally, the Poisson process is defined by three assumptions:\n\n1. **Assumption 1**: The number of occurrences in disjoint time intervals are independent.  \n   > Example: The number of calls received in the first hour does not affect the number of calls received in the second hour.\n\n2. **Assumption 2**: The probability of a single occurrence in a small time interval is proportional to the length of the interval.  \n   > Example: If you observe a toll booth for 10 minutes, the probability of a car passing through is proportional to those 10 minutes.\n\n3. **Assumption 3**: The probability of more than one occurrence in a small time interval is negligible.  \n   > Example: In a very short time frame (e.g., 1 second), it is highly unlikely that two cars pass through the toll booth simultaneously.\n\n### Probability Mass Function (PMF)\n\nThe number of occurrences in a time interval of length $t$ is denoted as $X(t)$. The PMF of $X(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nP(X(t) = n) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda t} (\\lambda t)^n}{n!}, \\quad \\text{for } n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots, \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\n| Symbol   | What it means                                | Typical units/range       |\n|----------|---------------------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $P(X(t) = n)$ | Probability of $n$ occurrences in time $t$ | Dimensionless (0 to 1) |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate of occurrences per unit time | Occurrences per unit time |\n| $t$ | Length of the time interval | Time (e.g., seconds, hours) |\n| $n$ | Number of occurrences | Non-negative integers |\n\nThis formula says that the probability of observing $n$ occurrences in a time interval $t$ depends on the rate $\\lambda$ and the interval length $t$. The term $e^{-\\lambda t}$ ensures that probabilities sum to 1.\n\n#### Example: Calculating PMF\n\nSuppose the rate of occurrences $\\lambda = 2$ events per hour, and we want to find the probability of observing exactly 3 events in a 2-hour interval.\n\n$$\nP(X(2) = 3) = \\frac{e^{-2 \\cdot 2} (2 \\cdot 2)^3}{3!}\n$$\n\nStep-by-step calculation:\n\n1. Compute $e^{-\\lambda t}$:  \n   $e^{-2 \\cdot 2} = e^{-4} \\approx 0.0183$\n\n2. Compute $(\\lambda t)^n$:  \n   $(2 \\cdot 2)^3 = 8^3 = 512$\n\n3. Compute $n!$:  \n   $3! = 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6$\n\n4. Combine terms:  \n   $P(X(2) = 3) = \\frac{0.0183 \\cdot 512}{6} \\approx 1.56$\n\nThus, the probability of observing exactly 3 events in 2 hours is approximately $0.156$.\n\n### Expectation and Variance\n\nThe expectation (mean) and variance of $X(t)$ are:\n\n$$\nE(X(t)) = \\lambda t\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X(t)) = \\lambda t\n$$\n\n| Symbol   | What it means                                | Typical units/range       |\n|----------|---------------------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $E(X(t))$ | Expected number of occurrences | Non-negative real numbers |\n| $\\text{Var}(X(t))$ | Variance of occurrences | Non-negative real numbers |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate of occurrences per unit time | Occurrences per unit time |\n| $t$ | Length of the time interval | Time (e.g., seconds, hours) |\n\n#### Example: Calculating Expectation and Variance\n\nIf $\\lambda = 5$ occurrences per hour and $t = 3$ hours:\n\n1. Compute expectation:  \n   $E(X(3)) = \\lambda t = 5 \\cdot 3 = 15$\n\n2. Compute variance:  \n   $\\text{Var}(X(3)) = \\lambda t = 5 \\cdot 3 = 15$\n\nThus, the expected number of occurrences in 3 hours is 15, and the variance is also 15.\n\n### Moment Generating Function (MGF)\n\nThe moment generating function (MGF) of $X(t)$ is:\n\n$$\nM(t) = e^{\\lambda (e^t - 1)}\n$$\n\n| Symbol   | What it means                                | Typical units/range       |\n|----------|---------------------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $M(t)$ | Moment generating function | Dimensionless |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate of occurrences per unit time | Occurrences per unit time |\n| $t$ | Moment parameter | Real numbers |\n\nThe MGF is used to derive moments (e.g., mean, variance) of the distribution.\n\n### Special Case: Poisson Distribution\n\nThe Poisson distribution is a special case of the Poisson process when $\\lambda t$ is constant. In this case, the PMF simplifies to:\n\n$$\nP(X = n) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda} \\lambda^n}{n!}, \\quad \\text{for } n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots, \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nThis is commonly used to model the number of occurrences in a fixed time interval.\n\n### Remarks on Assumptions\n\n1. **Assumption 2**: The probability of a single occurrence in a small interval $h$ is $P(X(h) = 1) = \\lambda h$. Here, $\\lambda$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\n2. **Assumption 3**: The probability of more than one occurrence in a small interval $h$ is negligible:\n\n   $$\n   P(X(h) \\geq 2) = 1 - P(X(h) = 0) - P(X(h) = 1) = \\epsilon(h)\n   $$\n\n   where $\\epsilon(h)$ is a negligible function.\n\n#### Definition of Negligible Function\n\nA function $f: \\mathbb{N} \\to [0, 1]$ is negligible if for all positive polynomials $p$, there exists $n_0 \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that for all $n \\geq n_0$:\n\n$$\nf(n) \\leq \\frac{1}{p(n)}\n$$\n\nThis means $f$ becomes asymptotically smaller than the inverse of any polynomial.\n\n### Exam Tip\n\nThe most-tested fact about the Poisson process is the PMF formula:\n\n$$\nP(X(t) = n) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda t} (\\lambda t)^n}{n!}\n$$\n\nCommon mistakes include forgetting to multiply $\\lambda$ by $t$ or incorrectly calculating $n!$. Always double-check these steps in calculations.\n\n## Uniform Continuous Distribution\n\n### Introduction to Uniform Continuous Distribution\nSimply put, a uniform continuous distribution models a situation where a random variable has an equal probability of taking any value within a specified interval. This interval is defined by two numbers, $a$ and $b$, which represent the lower and upper bounds, respectively.\n\n> Imagine rolling a perfectly balanced die, but instead of discrete numbers, the die can land anywhere between 1 and 6. Every number between 1 and 6 is equally likely to occur. This is similar to a uniform continuous distribution over the interval [1, 6].\n\nFormally, a random variable $X$ follows a uniform continuous distribution over the interval $[a, b]$ if its probability density function (PDF) is defined as:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a \\leq x \\leq b \\\\ \n0, & \\text{otherwise} \n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                     | Typical units/range        |\n|--------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| $x$ | Value of the random variable | Any real number |\n| $a$ | Lower bound of the interval | Real number ($a < b$) |\n| $b$ | Upper bound of the interval | Real number ($b > a$) |\n| $f(x)$ | Probability density function value | Probability per unit length |\n\nThis formula states that the probability density is constant ($\\frac{1}{b-a}$) for values of $x$ within the interval $[a, b]$, and zero outside this interval.\n\n### Validity of the PDF\nTo confirm that $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function, it must integrate to 1 over the interval $[a, b]$. The calculation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx = \\int_a^b \\frac{1}{b-a} \\, dx = \\frac{1}{b-a} \\int_a^b 1 \\, dx = \\frac{1}{b-a} \\cdot (b-a) = 1\n$$\n\nThus, the PDF is valid.\n\n### Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF)\nThe cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ gives the probability that the random variable $X$ is less than or equal to a given value $x$. For the uniform distribution, the CDF is defined as:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \n\\begin{cases} \n0, & x < a \\\\ \n\\frac{x-a}{b-a}, & a \\leq x \\leq b \\\\ \n1, & x > b \n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                     | Typical units/range        |\n|--------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| $x$ | Value of the random variable | Any real number |\n| $a$ | Lower bound of the interval | Real number ($a < b$) |\n| $b$ | Upper bound of the interval | Real number ($b > a$) |\n| $F(x)$ | Cumulative probability | Probability (0 to 1) |\n\nThis formula breaks into three cases:\n1. If $x < a$, the probability is 0 because $x$ is outside the interval.\n2. If $a \\leq x \\leq b$, the probability is proportional to how far $x$ is from $a$, scaled by the interval length $b-a$.\n3. If $x > b$, the probability is 1 because $x$ exceeds the upper bound, meaning all possible values of $X$ are less than or equal to $x$.\n\n### Expectation (Mean)\nThe expectation (mean) of a uniform continuous random variable $X$ is:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                     | Typical units/range        |\n|--------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| $E(X)$ | Expected value (mean) | Real number |\n| $a$ | Lower bound of the interval | Real number ($a < b$) |\n| $b$ | Upper bound of the interval | Real number ($b > a$) |\n\nThis formula calculates the midpoint of the interval $[a, b]$, which makes sense because the uniform distribution is symmetric.\n\n**Example:**\nLet $X \\sim \\text{Unif}(2, 8)$. Compute $E(X)$:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{2+8}{2} = \\frac{10}{2} = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the mean is 5.\n\n### Variance\nThe variance of a uniform continuous random variable $X$ is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\n| Symbol       | What it means                     | Typical units/range        |\n|--------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| $\\text{Var}(X)$ | Variance of $X$ | Real number (non-negative) |\n| $a$ | Lower bound of the interval | Real number ($a < b$) |\n| $b$ | Upper bound of the interval | Real number ($b > a$) |\n\nThis formula measures the spread of the distribution. Larger intervals result in higher variance.\n\n**Example:**\nLet $X \\sim \\text{Unif}(2, 8)$. Compute $\\text{Var}(X)$:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{(8-2)^2}{12} = \\frac{6^2}{12} = \\frac{36}{12} = 3\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is 3.\n\n### Moment Generating Function (MGF)\nThe moment generating function $M(t)$ for a uniform continuous random variable $X$ is:\n\n$$\nM(t) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\frac{e^{tb} - e^{ta}}{t(b-a)}, & t \\neq 0 \\\\ \n1, & t = 0 \n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                     | Typical units/range        |\n|--------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| $M(t)$ | Moment generating function | Function of $t$ |\n| $t$ | Parameter | Real number |\n| $a$ | Lower bound of the interval | Real number ($a < b$) |\n| $b$ | Upper bound of the interval | Real number ($b > a$) |\n\nThis formula helps compute moments of the distribution. For $t = 0$, $M(t)$ simplifies to 1 because $e^{0} = 1$.\n\n**Example:**\nLet $X \\sim \\text{Unif}(2, 8)$ and compute $M(t)$ for $t = 1$:\n\n$$\nM(1) = \\frac{e^{1 \\cdot 8} - e^{1 \\cdot 2}}{1 \\cdot (8-2)} = \\frac{e^8 - e^2}{6}\n$$\n\nNumerical evaluation would require approximations for $e^8$ and $e^2$.\n\n---\n\n> **Exam Tip:** The most common mistake on exams is forgetting that the PDF is constant only within the interval $[a, b]$. Always check the bounds before applying formulas!\n\n## Exponential Distribution\n\nThe exponential distribution is a fundamental probability distribution used to model the time until the first occurrence of an event in a Poisson process.\n\n### Definition and Real-World Analogy\n\nSimply put, the exponential distribution describes the time between events in a process where events happen continuously and independently at a constant average rate.\n\n> Imagine you're waiting for a bus at a stop where buses arrive randomly but at an average rate of 4 buses per hour. The time you wait for the next bus can be modeled using the exponential distribution.\n\nFormally, if a random variable $T$ follows the exponential distribution with parameter $\\lambda > 0$, we write $T \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$.\n\n### Probability Density Function (PDF)\n\nThe PDF of the exponential distribution is given by:\n\n$$\nf(t) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}, & t > 0 \\\\\n0, & t \\leq 0\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                  | Typical units/range       |\n|--------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $t$ | Time | $t > 0$ (seconds, hours) |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate parameter (events per unit time) | $\\lambda > 0$ |\n\nThis formula says that the probability density decreases exponentially as time $t$ increases. The parameter $\\lambda$ controls how quickly the probability density decreases.\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$ (events per hour). Find $f(t)$ for $t = 1$ hour.\n\n$$\nf(1) = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda t} = 2 e^{-2 \\cdot 1} = 2 e^{-2} \\approx 2 \\cdot 0.1353 = 0.2706\n$$\n\nSo, the probability density at $t = 1$ is approximately $0.2706$.\n\n### Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF)\n\nThe CDF of the exponential distribution is:\n\n$$\nF(t) = \n\\begin{cases} \n1 - e^{-\\lambda t}, & t > 0 \\\\\n0, & t \\leq 0\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                  | Typical units/range       |\n|--------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $t$ | Time | $t > 0$ (seconds, hours) |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate parameter (events per unit time) | $\\lambda > 0$ |\n\nThis formula gives the probability that the time until the event occurs is less than or equal to $t$. \n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$ (events per hour). Find $F(t)$ for $t = 1$ hour.\n\n$$\nF(1) = 1 - e^{-\\lambda t} = 1 - e^{-2 \\cdot 1} = 1 - e^{-2} \\approx 1 - 0.1353 = 0.8647\n$$\n\nSo, the probability that the event occurs within 1 hour is approximately $0.8647$.\n\n### Expectation and Variance\n\nThe mean (expected value) and variance of an exponential random variable $T \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ are:\n\n#### Expectation:\n\n$$\nE(T) = \\frac{1}{\\lambda}\n$$\n\n#### Variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(T) = \\frac{1}{\\lambda^2}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                  | Typical units/range       |\n|--------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate parameter (events per unit time) | $\\lambda > 0$ |\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$ (events per hour). Find $E(T)$ and $\\text{Var}(T)$.\n\n$$\nE(T) = \\frac{1}{\\lambda} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5 \\, \\text{hours}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(T) = \\frac{1}{\\lambda^2} = \\frac{1}{2^2} = \\frac{1}{4} = 0.25 \\, \\text{hours}^2\n$$\n\nSo, the mean waiting time is $0.5$ hours, and the variance is $0.25$ hours$^2$.\n\n### Moment Generating Function (MGF)\n\nThe moment generating function (MGF) of the exponential distribution is:\n\n$$\nM(u) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda - u}, & u < \\lambda \\\\\n1, & u = 0\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                  | Typical units/range       |\n|--------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $u$ | Argument of the MGF | $u < \\lambda$ |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate parameter (events per unit time) | $\\lambda > 0$ |\n\nThe MGF is useful for calculating moments (mean, variance, etc.) of the distribution.\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$ and $u = 1$. Find $M(u)$.\n\n$$\nM(1) = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda - u} = \\frac{2}{2 - 1} = 2\n$$\n\nSo, the MGF at $u = 1$ is $2$.\n\n### Memoryless Property\n\nThe exponential distribution has a unique property called the memoryless property:\n\n$$\nP(T > a + b \\mid T > b) = P(T > a)\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means                  | Typical units/range       |\n|--------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| $T$ | Time until the event occurs | $T > 0$ |\n| $a, b$ | Time intervals | $a, b > 0$ |\n\nThis property means that the probability of waiting an additional time $a$, given that you've already waited $b$, is the same as the probability of waiting $a$ from the start. In other words, the exponential distribution \"forgets\" how much time has already passed.\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$. Find $P(T > 3 \\mid T > 1)$.\n\nUsing the memoryless property:\n\n$$\nP(T > 3 \\mid T > 1) = P(T > 3 - 1) = P(T > 2)\n$$\n\n$$\nP(T > 2) = e^{-\\lambda t} = e^{-2 \\cdot 2} = e^{-4} \\approx 0.0183\n$$\n\nSo, the probability is approximately $0.0183$.\n\n### Gamma Function and Higher-Order Expectations\n\nThe $k$-th order expectation of $T \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ is:\n\n$$\nE(T^k) = \\frac{\\Gamma(k+1)}{\\lambda^k}\n$$\n\nWhere $\\Gamma(k+1)$ is the Gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(k+1) = \\int_0^\\infty x^k e^{-x} dx\n$$\n\nFor positive integers $k$, $\\Gamma(k+1) = k!$.\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$ and $k = 1$. Find $E(T^1)$.\n\n$$\nE(T^1) = \\frac{\\Gamma(1+1)}{\\lambda^1} = \\frac{\\Gamma(2)}{2} = \\frac{1!}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, the first-order expectation is $0.5$ hours.\n\n### Proposition: Minimum of Independent Exponential Random Variables\n\nIf $X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_n$ are independent exponential random variables with parameters $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2, \\dots, \\lambda_n$, then:\n\n$$\n\\text{min}(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_n) \\sim \\text{Exp}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i\\right)\n$$\n\nThis result is useful for modeling systems with competing exponential processes.\n\n---\n\n> **Exam Tip:** The memoryless property is the most commonly tested feature of the exponential distribution. Make sure you understand how to apply it to conditional probabilities.\n\n## Gamma Distribution\n\n### What is the Gamma Distribution?\n\nThe Gamma distribution is a continuous probability distribution that models the time until the $r$-th occurrence in a Poisson process. In a Poisson process, random events occur independently and continuously over time, such as the arrival of customers at a store or the occurrence of phone calls in a call center.\n\nFormally, if $T$ is the time until the $r$-th event in a Poisson process with rate $\\lambda > 0$, then $T$ follows a Gamma distribution with shape and rate parameters $(\\lambda, r)$ and is denoted as:\n\n$$\nT \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\lambda, r).\n$$\n\n> Example: Imagine you're waiting for buses at a bus stop, and buses arrive at random intervals. The Gamma distribution can be used to model how long you'll need to wait for the $r$-th bus.\n\n---\n\n### Relationship Between Gamma and Chi-Square Distribution\n\nThe Chi-square distribution is a **special case of the Gamma distribution**. Specifically:\n\n- If $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $r = \\frac{n}{2}$ (where $n$ is a positive integer), then:\n\n  $$\n  T \\sim \\text{Gamma}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{n}{2}\\right)\n  $$\n\n  becomes equivalent to the Chi-square distribution with $n$ degrees of freedom:\n\n  $$\n  T \\sim \\chi_n^2.\n  $$\n\n#### Why does this work? 💡 \nA Chi-square random variable arises from the sum of the squares of $n$ independent standard normal random variables. It can also be shown (see source slides) that the square of a standard normal random variable corresponds to an exponential random variable with parameter $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{2}$. Summing $n$ such squared terms creates a Gamma distribution with specific parameters — the Chi-square distribution.\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** Be careful not to confuse Chi-square and Gamma parameters. For a Chi-square random variable with $n$ degrees of freedom, $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $r = \\frac{n}{2}$, **not** $\\lambda = 1$ and $r = n$.\n\n---\n\n### Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF)\n\nThe CDF of a Gamma random variable $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nF(t) = \n\\begin{cases} \n1 - \\sum_{j=0}^{r-1} \\frac{e^{-\\lambda t} (\\lambda t)^j}{j!}, & t > 0 \\\\\n0, & t \\leq 0\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThis gives the probability that $T$, the time until the $r$-th event, is less than or equal to $t$. \n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| $F(t)$ | Cumulative probability up to time $t$ | $[0, 1]$ |\n| $t$ | Time variable | $t > 0$ |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate of the Poisson process | $\\lambda > 0$ |\n| $r$ | Number of occurrences | $r \\in \\mathbb{N}$ |\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$, $r = 3$, and $t = 1$. Compute $F(t)$:\n\n1. Evaluate the cumulative sum:\n\n   - For $j = 0$: \n\n     $$\n     \\frac{e^{-2 \\cdot 1} (2 \\cdot 1)^0}{0!} = e^{-2}.\n     $$\n\n   - For $j = 1$:\n\n     $$\n     \\frac{e^{-2 \\cdot 1} (2 \\cdot 1)^1}{1!} = 2e^{-2}.\n     $$\n\n   - For $j = 2$: \n\n     $$\n     \\frac{e^{-2 \\cdot 1} (2 \\cdot 1)^2}{2!} = 2e^{-2}.\n     $$\n\n2. Sum terms: \n\n   $$\n   e^{-2} + 2e^{-2} + 2e^{-2} = 5e^{-2}.\n   $$\n\n3. Subtract from 1: \n\n   $$\n   F(1) = 1 - 5e^{-2} \\approx 0.6767.\n   $$\n\n---\n\n### Probability Density Function (PDF)\n\nThe PDF of a Gamma random variable $T$ is:\n\n$$\nf(t) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\frac{\\lambda^r t^{r-1} e^{-\\lambda t}}{\\Gamma(r)}, & t > 0 \\\\\n0, & t \\leq 0\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe Gamma function $\\Gamma(r)$ generalizes the factorial and is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(r) = \\int_0^\\infty x^{r-1} e^{-x} dx.\n$$\n\nFor integers, $\\Gamma(r) = (r - 1)!$.\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Units/range |\n|--------|---------------|-------------|\n| $f(t)$ | Probability density at time $t$ | $[0, \\infty)$ |\n| $t$ | Time | $t > 0$ |\n| $\\lambda$ | Rate | $\\lambda > 0$ |\n| $r$ | Shape (number of occurrences) | $r > 0$ |\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$, $r = 3$, and $t = 1$. Compute $f(t)$:\n\n- Compute $\\Gamma(r)$: \n\n  $$\n  \\Gamma(3) = (3 - 1)! = 2! = 2.\n  $$\n\n- Substitute into the PDF formula:  \n\n  $$\n  f(1) = \\frac{2^3 \\cdot 1^{3-1} \\cdot e^{-2 \\cdot 1}}{\\Gamma(3)} = \\frac{8 \\cdot e^{-2}}{2} = 4e^{-2}.\n  $$\n\n- Approximate: \n\n  $$\n  f(1) \\approx 4 \\cdot 0.1353 = 0.5412.\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Expectation and Variance\n\nThe expectation (mean) and variance of a Gamma random variable $T$ are:\n\n$$\nE(T) = \\frac{r}{\\lambda}, \\quad \\text{Var}(T) = \\frac{r}{\\lambda^2}.\n$$\n\n| Symbol | Description | Formula |\n|--------|-------------|---------|\n| $E(T)$ | Expected time | $\\frac{r}{\\lambda}$ |\n| $\\text{Var}(T)$ | Variance of time | $\\frac{r}{\\lambda^2}$ |\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$ and $r = 3$:\n\n- Expectation: \n\n  $$\n  E(T) = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5.\n  $$\n\n- Variance:  \n\n  $$\n  \\text{Var}(T) = \\frac{3}{2^2} = 0.75.\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Moment Generating Function (MGF)\n\nThe moment generating function (MGF) of a Gamma random variable $T$ is:\n\n$$\nM(u) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda - u}\\right)^r, & u < \\lambda \\\\\n1, & u = 0\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThis is useful for deriving moments like the mean and variance.\n\n#### Example:\nLet $\\lambda = 2$, $r = 3$, and $u = 1$. Compute $M(u)$:\n\n- Substitute values:  \n\n  $$\n  M(1) = \\left(\\frac{2}{2 - 1}\\right)^3 = 2^3 = 8.\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Special Properties\n\n1. **Additivity**: If $X_1 \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\lambda, r_1)$ and $X_2 \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\lambda, r_2)$ are independent, then:\n\n   $$\n   X_1 + X_2 \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\lambda, r_1 + r_2).\n   $$\n\n2. **Connection to Exponentials**: If $T \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\lambda, 1)$, then $T$ is an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda$.\n\n---\n\n### Chi-Square Wrap-Up 💡\n\nWhen faced with Chi-square and Gamma distributions:\n- Recall that $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $r = \\frac{n}{2}$ for a Chi-square distribution with $n$ degrees of freedom.\n- Misattributing $\\lambda = 1$ and $r = n$ for a Chi-square leads to incorrect conclusions.\n\nThis equivalence links the Chi-square distribution to the Gamma family and highlights the versatility of the Gamma distribution in modeling various phenomena.\n\n---\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** On exams, use $\\chi^2$ distributions for sums of squares in normal variables and verify whether $r$ and $\\lambda$ conform to the Chi-square's definition.\n\n## Beta Distribution\n\n### What is the Beta Distribution?\n\nSimply put, the Beta distribution is a probability distribution that models random variables within the interval [0, 1]. It is particularly useful for representing probabilities or proportions, such as the likelihood of success in a given scenario.\n\n> Imagine you are flipping a coin and want to model the probability that the coin lands heads. The Beta distribution can describe this probability, considering how many times you've flipped the coin and how many heads you've observed.\n\nFormally, a random variable $X$ follows the Beta distribution with parameters $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ (where $\\alpha, \\beta > 0$) if its probability density function (PDF) is given by:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \n\\begin{cases} \n\\frac{x^{\\alpha-1}(1-x)^{\\beta-1}}{B(\\alpha, \\beta)}, & 0 < x < 1 \\\\ \n0, & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $f(x)$ | Probability density function | Dimensionless |\n| $x$ | Random variable | $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ |\n| $\\alpha$ | Shape parameter 1 | $\\alpha > 0$ |\n| $\\beta$ | Shape parameter 2 | $\\beta > 0$ |\n| $B(\\alpha, \\beta)$ | Beta function (normalizing constant) | Dimensionless |\n\nThis formula says that the PDF is proportional to $x^{\\alpha-1}(1-x)^{\\beta-1}$ for values of $x$ between 0 and 1, and zero otherwise. The Beta function $B(\\alpha, \\beta)$ ensures that the total area under the curve equals 1, as required for a probability density function.\n\n#### Special Case: Uniform Distribution\nWhen $\\alpha = \\beta = 1$, the Beta distribution simplifies to the uniform distribution over the interval [0, 1]. This means that every value in the interval is equally likely.\n\n### Beta Function\n\nThe Beta function $B(\\alpha, \\beta)$ is a key component of the Beta distribution and is defined as:\n\n$$\nB(\\alpha, \\beta) = \\int_0^1 x^{\\alpha-1}(1-x)^{\\beta-1} \\, dx\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $B(\\alpha, \\beta)$ | Beta function | Dimensionless |\n| $\\alpha$ | Shape parameter 1 | $\\alpha > 0$ |\n| $\\beta$ | Shape parameter 2 | $\\beta > 0$ |\n\nThe Beta function acts as a normalizing constant to ensure that the total probability across the interval [0, 1] sums to 1. It can also be expressed in terms of the Gamma function:\n\n$$\nB(\\alpha, \\beta) = \\frac{\\Gamma(\\alpha) \\Gamma(\\beta)}{\\Gamma(\\alpha + \\beta)}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $\\Gamma(\\alpha)$ | Gamma function | Dimensionless |\n| $\\Gamma(\\beta)$ | Gamma function | Dimensionless |\n| $\\Gamma(\\alpha + \\beta)$ | Gamma function | Dimensionless |\n\nThis relationship allows us to compute $B(\\alpha, \\beta)$ without directly evaluating the integral.\n\n#### Numerical Example: Computing $B(2, 3)$\nUsing the Gamma function relationship:\n\n$$\nB(2, 3) = \\frac{\\Gamma(2) \\Gamma(3)}{\\Gamma(2 + 3)}\n$$\n\nFrom the properties of the Gamma function:\n- $\\Gamma(2) = 1! = 1$\n- $\\Gamma(3) = 2! = 2$\n- $\\Gamma(5) = 4! = 24$\n\nSubstitute these values:\n\n$$\nB(2, 3) = \\frac{1 \\cdot 2}{24} = \\frac{2}{24} = \\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nThus, $B(2, 3) = \\frac{1}{12}$.\n\n### Expectation and Variance\n\n#### Expectation\nThe expected value (mean) of a Beta random variable $X$ is given by:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha + \\beta}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $E(X)$ | Expected value (mean) | Dimensionless |\n| $\\alpha$ | Shape parameter 1 | $\\alpha > 0$ |\n| $\\beta$ | Shape parameter 2 | $\\beta > 0$ |\n\nThis formula shows that the mean depends on the relative sizes of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. If $\\alpha = \\beta$, the mean is 0.5 (the midpoint of the interval [0, 1]).\n\n#### Variance\nThe variance of a Beta random variable $X$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{\\alpha \\beta}{(\\alpha + \\beta)^2 (\\alpha + \\beta + 1)}\n$$\n\n| Symbol | What it means | Typical units/range |\n|--------|---------------|---------------------|\n| $\\text{Var}(X)$ | Variance | Dimensionless |\n| $\\alpha$ | Shape parameter 1 | $\\alpha > 0$ |\n| $\\beta$ | Shape parameter 2 | $\\beta > 0$ |\n\nThis formula shows that the variance decreases as $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ increase, meaning the distribution becomes more concentrated around the mean.\n\n#### Numerical Example: Computing $E(X)$ and $\\text{Var}(X)$ for $\\alpha = 2$, $\\beta = 3$\n1. Compute $E(X)$:\n\n   $$\n   E(X) = \\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha + \\beta} = \\frac{2}{2 + 3} = \\frac{2}{5} = 0.4\n   $$\n\n2. Compute $\\text{Var}(X)$:\n\n   $$\n   \\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{\\alpha \\beta}{(\\alpha + \\beta)^2 (\\alpha + \\beta + 1)}\n   $$\n\n   Substitute $\\alpha = 2$, $\\beta = 3$:\n\n   $$\n   \\text{Var}(X) = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{(2 + 3)^2 (2 + 3 + 1)} = \\frac{6}{5^2 \\cdot 6} = \\frac{6}{150} = 0.04\n   $$\n\nThus, $E(X) = 0.4$ and $\\text{Var}(X) = 0.04$.\n\n### Higher-Order Expectations\n\nThe $k$-th order expectation of a Beta random variable $X$ is given by:\n\n$$\nE(X^k) = \\frac{B(\\alpha + k, \\beta)}{B(\\alpha, \\beta)}\n$$\n\nUsing the Gamma function relationship:\n\n$$\nE(X^k) = \\frac{\\Gamma(\\alpha + k) \\Gamma(\\beta)}{\\Gamma(\\alpha + \\beta + k)} \\cdot \\frac{\\Gamma(\\alpha + \\beta)}{\\Gamma(\\alpha) \\Gamma(\\beta)}\n$$\n\nThis formula allows us to compute moments of the Beta distribution.\n\n#### Special Case: First-Order Expectation\nFor $k = 1$, the formula reduces to:\n\n$$\nE(X) = \\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha + \\beta}\n$$\n\nThis matches the earlier formula for the mean.\n\n### Visualizing the Beta Distribution\n\nThe shape of the Beta distribution depends on the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. For example:\n- If $\\alpha > \\beta$, the distribution is skewed to the right.\n- If $\\alpha < \\beta$, the distribution is skewed to the left.\n- If $\\alpha = \\beta$, the distribution is symmetric.\n\n#### Exercise: Plot Shapes for Different $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$\nTry plotting the PDF for:\n1. $\\alpha = 2$, $\\beta = 5$\n2. $\\alpha = 5$, $\\beta = 2$\n3. $\\alpha = \\beta = 3$\n\nThis will help you understand how $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ affect the distribution.\n\n---\n\n> **Exam Tip:** The most common mistake students make is forgetting that the Beta distribution is defined only on the interval [0, 1]. Always check the range of $x$ when solving problems!\n\n## Normal (Gaussian) Distribution\n\n### What is the Normal Distribution?\n\nThe normal distribution is a fundamental concept in probability and statistics, used to model continuous random variables that tend to cluster around a central value, forming a symmetric, bell-shaped curve. It is one of the most widely used probability distributions in various fields.\n\n> For example, consider measuring the heights of all students in a school. Most students will have heights close to the average, while very few will be extremely short or tall. This clustering around the average height forms a bell-shaped pattern, which is modeled by the normal distribution.\n\n### Formal Definition\n\nA random variable $X$ is said to follow a normal (or Gaussian) distribution with parameters $\\mu$ (mean) and $\\sigma^2$ (variance) if its probability density function (PDF) is given by:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right), \\quad -\\infty < x < \\infty\n$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Typical Units/Range |\n|--------|---------|----------------------|\n| $x$ | Value of the random variable | Any real number |\n| $\\mu$ | Mean (average) of the distribution | Any real number |\n| $\\sigma^2$ | Variance (spread of the data) | Positive real number |\n| $\\sigma$ | Standard deviation ($\\sqrt{\\sigma^2}$) | Positive real number |\n\nThis formula indicates that the probability density of $X$ at a specific value $x$ depends on the distance of $x$ from the mean $\\mu$, scaled by the standard deviation $\\sigma$. The exponential term ensures that values closer to $\\mu$ have higher probabilities, while values farther away have lower probabilities.\n\n### Properties of the Normal Distribution\n\n1. **Expectation (Mean):** The mean of $X$ is $\\mu$:\n\n   $$\n   E[X] = \\mu\n   $$\n\n2. **Variance:** The variance of $X$ is $\\sigma^2$:\n\n   $$\n   \\text{Var}(X) = \\sigma^2\n   $$\n\n3. **Standard Normal Distribution:** When $\\mu = 0$ and $\\sigma^2 = 1$, the normal distribution is referred to as the standard normal distribution. Its PDF simplifies to:\n\n   $$\n   f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)\n   $$\n\n4. **Sum of Independent Normal Random Variables:** If $X_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2 \\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)$ are independent, then their sum $X_1 + X_2$ also follows a normal distribution:\n\n   $$\n   X_1 + X_2 \\sim N(\\mu_1 + \\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2)\n   $$\n\n### Validity of the PDF\n\nFor $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function, it must integrate to 1 over the entire real line. This is verified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, dx = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right) \\, dx\n$$\n\nBy substituting $z = \\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$, the integral becomes:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{z^2}{2}\\right) \\, dz\n$$\n\nThis is a standard Gaussian integral, and its value is 1. Therefore, the PDF is valid.\n\n### Computing Expectation and Variance\n\n#### Expectation:\nTo compute $E[X]$, we use the property that the standardized variable $\\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}$ has an expectation of 0 (due to symmetry about $\\mu$). This gives:\n\n$$\nE[X] = \\mu\n$$\n\n#### Variance:\nThe variance is derived using the second moment:\n\n$$\nE[X^2] = \\sigma^2 + \\mu^2\n$$\n\nThus:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2 = \\sigma^2\n$$\n\n### Standard Normal Distribution and Z-Transformation\n\nIf $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we can standardize $X$ using the transformation:\n\n$$\nZ = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nThe resulting variable $Z$ follows a standard normal distribution with mean 0 and variance 1:\n\n$$\nZ \\sim N(0, 1)\n$$\n\nThis transformation simplifies probability calculations. For example:\n- To find $P(X < b)$, note that $X < b$ is equivalent to $Z < \\frac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma}$.\n- Similarly, for $a < X < b$, we compute:\n\n$$\nP(a < X < b) = P\\left(\\frac{a-\\mu}{\\sigma} < Z < \\frac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)\n$$\n\n### Worked Example: Standardizing a Normal Variable\n\nSuppose $X \\sim N(50, 16)$ (mean = 50, variance = 16). Find $P(X < 54)$.\n\n1. Standardize $X$:\n\n   $$\n   Z = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma} = \\frac{X - 50}{4}\n   $$\n\n2. For $X < 54$, compute $Z$:\n\n   $$\n   Z = \\frac{54 - 50}{4} = 1\n   $$\n\n3. Use the standard normal table to find $P(Z < 1)$. From the table:\n\n   $$\n   P(Z < 1) \\approx 0.8413\n   $$\n\nThus, $P(X < 54) = 0.8413$.\n\n### Special Case: Chi-Squared Distribution\n\nWhen the squares of independent standard normal variables are summed, the result follows a chi-squared distribution. For example, if $Z_1$ and $Z_2$ are independent standard normal variables, then:\n\n$$\nD^2 = Z_1^2 + Z_2^2\n$$\n\nfollows a chi-squared distribution with 2 degrees of freedom.\n\n#### Example:\nIf $D^2 > 9$, the probability is:\n\n$$\nP(D^2 > 9) = P(\\chi^2_2 > 9) \\approx 0.0111\n$$\n\n> 📝 **Exam Tip:** The most common exam mistake is forgetting to standardize a normal variable before using the standard normal table. Always apply the transformation $Z = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}$ first!",
