    "name": "Digital Signal Processing",
    "course": "EC301 — DSP",
    "note": "```markdown\n## Fourier Transform\n\nThe Fourier Transform is a mathematical tool used to analyze and represent functions or signals in terms of their frequency components. It transforms a time-domain signal into its frequency-domain representation, providing insight into the signal's spectral characteristics.\n\n### Definition\n\nFor a continuous-time signal $f(t)$, the Fourier Transform $F(\\omega)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nF(\\omega) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(t) e^{-i \\omega t} \\, dt\n$$\n\nHere:\n- $f(t)$ is the original signal in the time domain.\n- $F(\\omega)$ is the transformed signal in the frequency domain.\n- $\\omega$ represents angular frequency, measured in radians per second.\n- $i$ is the imaginary unit ($i^2 = -1$).\n\nThe inverse Fourier Transform allows us to reconstruct the original signal $f(t)$ from its frequency-domain representation $F(\\omega)$:\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty F(\\omega) e^{i \\omega t} \\, d\\omega\n$$\n\n### Intuition\n\nThe Fourier Transform decomposes a signal into a sum of sinusoidal components (sines and cosines) of varying frequencies, amplitudes, and phases. Each frequency component corresponds to a \"building block\" of the original signal. This decomposition is particularly useful for understanding periodicity, filtering signals, and solving differential equations.\n\n### Key Properties\n\n1. **Linearity**: If $f_1(t)$ and $f_2(t)$ have Fourier Transforms $F_1(\\omega)$ and $F_2(\\omega)$, then for any constants $a$ and $b$:\n\n   $$\n   \\text{Fourier Transform of } af_1(t) + bf_2(t) = aF_1(\\omega) + bF_2(\\omega).\n   $$\n\n2. **Time Shift**: If $f(t)$ is shifted by $t_0$, its Fourier Transform becomes:\n\n   $$\n   f(t - t_0) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} F(\\omega)e^{-i \\omega t_0}.\n   $$\n\n3. **Frequency Shift**: Multiplying $f(t)$ by $e^{i \\omega_0 t}$ shifts its Fourier Transform:\n\n   $$\n   f(t)e^{i \\omega_0 t} \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} F(\\omega - \\omega_0).\n   $$\n\n4. **Scaling**: If $f(at)$ is a scaled version of $f(t)$, its Fourier Transform is scaled inversely:\n\n   $$\n   f(at) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} \\frac{1}{|a|} F\\left(\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right).\n   $$\n\n5. **Parseval's Theorem**: The total energy of a signal is preserved in both the time and frequency domains:\n\n   $$\n   \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty |f(t)|^2 \\, dt = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty |F(\\omega)|^2 \\, d\\omega.\n   $$\n\n### Applications\n\nThe Fourier Transform is widely used in fields such as signal processing, communications, image analysis, and physics. Common applications include:\n- Filtering unwanted frequencies from signals.\n- Analyzing the frequency content of audio and images.\n- Solving partial differential equations in engineering and physics.\n\nUnderstanding the Fourier Transform provides a foundation for working with more advanced tools like the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) and Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), which are computationally efficient implementations for discrete signals.\n```\n\n\n---\n\n**Amendment (page 1):**\n\n```markdown\n## Fourier Transform\n\nThe Fourier Transform is a mathematical tool used to analyze and represent functions or signals in terms of their frequency components. It transforms a time-domain signal into its frequency-domain representation, providing insight into the signal's spectral characteristics.\n\n### Definition\n\nFor a continuous-time signal $f(t)$, the Fourier Transform $F(\\omega)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nF(\\omega) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(t) e^{-i \\omega t} \\, dt\n$$\n\nHere:\n- $f(t)$ is the original signal in the time domain.\n- $F(\\omega)$ is the transformed signal in the frequency domain.\n- $\\omega$ represents angular frequency, measured in radians per second.\n- $i$ is the imaginary unit ($i^2 = -1$).\n\nThe inverse Fourier Transform reconstructs the original signal $f(t)$ from its frequency-domain representation $F(\\omega)$:\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty F(\\omega) e^{i \\omega t} \\, d\\omega\n$$\n\n### Intuition\n\nThe Fourier Transform expresses a signal as a sum of sinusoidal components (both sines and cosines) having various frequencies, amplitudes, and phases. These components act as \"building blocks\" of the signal, allowing us to transition from observing how a signal evolves over time (the time domain) to how it breaks down into frequencies (the frequency domain). Conversely, the inverse Fourier Transform combines these frequency components back into the original signal.\n\n💡 Intuition behind Duality: Duality showcases an inherent symmetry in the Fourier Transform. The roles of the time-domain signal $f(t)$ and its frequency-domain counterpart $F(\\omega)$ can be interchanged under specific modifications. This means that analyzing $f(t)$ as a function of time versus analyzing $F(\\omega)$ as a function of frequency are symmetric, complementary perspectives on the same data. This property allows techniques in one domain to be applied in the other.\n\n---\n\n### Key Properties\n\n1. **Linearity**  \n   The Fourier Transform is linear, meaning the transform of a scaled or summed signal is equal to the scaled or summed transforms:\n\n   $$\n   \\text{If } f_1(t) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} F_1(\\omega) \\text{ and } f_2(t) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} F_2(\\omega), \\text{ then }\n   $$\n\n   $$\n   af_1(t) + bf_2(t) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} aF_1(\\omega) + bF_2(\\omega).\n   $$\n\n2. **Time Shift**  \n   If the signal $f(t)$ is shifted in time by $t_0$, its Fourier Transform is multiplied by a complex exponential:\n\n   $$\n   f(t - t_0) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} F(\\omega)e^{-i \\omega t_0}.\n   $$\n\n3. **Frequency Shift**  \n   If $f(t)$ is modulated (multiplied by $e^{i \\omega_0 t}$), its Fourier Transform is shifted by $\\omega_0$:\n\n   $$\n   f(t)e^{i \\omega_0 t} \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} F(\\omega - \\omega_0).\n   $$\n\n4. **Scaling**  \n   A compression or expansion of $f(t)$ by a factor of $a$ scales its Fourier Transform inversely:\n\n   $$\n   f(at) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} \\frac{1}{|a|} F\\left(\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right).\n   $$\n\n5. **Parseval's Theorem**  \n   The energy of a signal is preserved across time and frequency domains:\n\n   $$\n   \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty |f(t)|^2 \\, dt = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty |F(\\omega)|^2 \\, d\\omega.\n   $$\n\n6. **Duality**  \n\n   Duality highlights that the Fourier Transform has symmetry between the time and frequency domains. Specifically, the Fourier Transform of $f(t)$ is $F(\\omega)$, and if we swap the roles of $f(t)$ and $F(\\omega)$ (time and frequency domain), we get a similar Fourier relation. Formally, if:\n\n   $$\n   f(t) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} F(\\omega),\n   $$\n\n   then under duality:\n\n   $$\n   \\frac{1}{2\\pi} F(t) \\xrightarrow{\\text{FT}} f(-\\omega).\n   $$\n\n   💡 Why it works: The mathematical derivation of duality rests on the symmetry of the Fourier kernel $e^{-i\\omega t}$ under role-reversal. Intuitively, it implies that operating in the time domain and frequency domain offers interchangeable interpretations of the same signal, connected by scaling factors.\n\n   > 📝 **Exam Tip:** If given a Fourier Transform pair $f(t) \\leftrightarrow F(\\omega)$, you can use duality to immediately deduce what $F(t)$ transforms into, without recalculating the Fourier integral.\n\n---\n\n### Applications\n\nThe Fourier Transform has extensive applications in science and engineering, including:\n- **Signal analysis**: Decomposing audio, radio, or video signals into their frequency content.\n- **Filtering**: Removing unwanted frequencies while preserving key components of a signal.\n- **Image processing**: Enhancing, compressing, or analyzing images via their frequency spectrum.\n- **Solving differential equations**: Transforming difficult equations into algebraic forms for easier solutions.\n\nUnderstanding the Fourier Transform sets the foundation for advanced tools like the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) and Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), which efficiently handle discrete signals in computational applications.\n```",
